    <div class="page">
        <header class="masthead">
            <div class="masthead-text">
                <h1>Methodology and notes</h1>
                <p class="lead">
                    What the figures measure, how they were assembled, and how
                    carefully they should be compared across years.
                </p>
            </div>
        </header>

        <div class="prose">
            <h2>What is measured</h2>
            <p>
                Each figure is the percentage of people aged 3 and over who
                reported, in that census, that they could speak Irish. It is
                self-reported ability, not a test of fluency and not a measure of
                how often the language is used.
            </p>

            <h2>Comparing across years</h2>
            <p>
                Census questions and definitions changed over time, so
                comparisons between distant years should be interpreted
                cautiously. The CSO notes that results such as 1926 and 2022 are
                not directly comparable, and a new question series began in 1996.
            </p>

            <h2>Northern Ireland</h2>
            <p>
                Northern Ireland recorded Irish only in the all-island censuses
                of 1851 to 1911 and again from 1991, so the six counties show as
                "no data" in between. Its 2001 and 2021 censuses appear under
                2002 and 2022 to line up with the Republic; the county card says
                so when that applies. Antrim and Down for 1851, 1861 and 1901 are
                estimated, because Belfast straddles both counties and was not
                split between them in those returns.
            </p>
            <p>
                Some Northern Ireland county figures are themselves estimates.
                The 1991, 2001 and 2011 values were derived from ward-level
                returns, and NISRA's 2021 county figures are estimated from
                census areas, as the North does not publish this on a county
                basis directly.
            </p>

            <h2>Sources</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Republic of Ireland: Central Statistics Office (<a href="https://data.cso.ie/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data.cso.ie</a>).</li>
                <li>Northern Ireland: the Northern Ireland censuses, compiled by <a href="https://bsky.app/profile/phelimbirch.bsky.social" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Phelim Birch</a>.</li>
                <li>Boundaries: Ordnance Survey Ireland and Ordnance Survey of Northern Ireland.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

